    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> were independent in all but name, yet paid the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> 800,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> a year and named him in the Friday khutba.</li><li>Their conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, begun in 827 under the qadi Asad ibn al-Furat, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in 831, Syracuse in 878 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> only in 902.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-i">Ziyadat Allah I</a> rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a> in 836; its minaret is the oldest surviving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and its teak minbar the oldest in the world.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Aghlabid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> were Mu'tazilites who held that the Qur'an was created, a doctrine the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> majority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> rejected; the scholar Sahsun was persecuted under Muhammad I for refusing it.</p></aside>
    </section>

