    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid.jpg" alt="Harun al-Rashid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Harun_al-Rashid,_AH_170-193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8/9th-century master(s), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 800 the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-i-ibn-al-aghlab">Ibrahim I ibn al-Aghlab</a>, the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasani</a> Arab commander of the Banu Tamim tribe, hereditary emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, in answer to the anarchy that had followed the fall of the Muhallabids in that province. Perhaps 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> then lived in Ifriqiya, most of them immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> remained the great majority. Ibrahim's charge covered what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and Tripolitania, and in theory the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, since the territory granted him was never demarcated. Though independent in all but name, the dynasty never ceased to recognise Abbasid overlordship: it paid an annual tribute of 800,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> and had the caliph's suzerainty proclaimed in the khutba at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a>.</p><p>After pacifying the country, Ibrahim founded a new capital, al-Abbasiyya, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in 800 and built it between 801 and 810. He did so partly to distance himself from the Malikite jurists and theologians, who condemned the luxurious life of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a>, disliked their Mu'tazilite theology and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> law, and objected to the unequal treatment of the Muslim Berbers. His line ruled for eleven reigns: Abdallah I (812–817), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-i">Ziyadat Allah I</a> (817–838), al-Aghlab, Muhammad I, Ahmad I, Ziyadat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> II, Muhammad II, Ibrahim II (875–902), Abdallah II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii">Ziyadat Allah III</a> (903–909).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of Kairouan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_Kairouan_Panorama_-_Grande_Mosqu%C3%A9e_de_Kairouan_Panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MAREK SZAREJKO from CLONMEL, IRELAND - POLAND, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> was a land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> cultivators ruled by an Arab minority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> built up the irrigation of the region and improved the public buildings and mosques of its towns; the Aghlabid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">Reservoirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> survive as an example of their practical works. Agriculture and the trans-Saharan trade were further developed under their rule, bringing economic expansion and a growing urban population, and the campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> that began in 827 brought new revenues to the state. Slaves were obtained through the trans-Saharan trade, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> commerce and from raids on Sicily and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; 5,000 black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> slaves are recorded as part of the garrison of al-Abbasiyya.</p><p>The court lived richly enough to draw the censure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> scholars, and the emirs answered by publicly dispensing charity to the poor and by sponsoring the construction and expansion of mosques. Their building combined Abbasid and Byzantine traditions into a style of their own, heavy and almost fortress-like even in its mosques. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a>, rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-i">Ziyadat Allah I</a> in 836, has an enormous courtyard, a hypostyle prayer hall and a thick three-storey minaret perhaps modelled on Roman lighthouses, the oldest surviving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>; its mihrab is faced with carved marble and lustre tiles and stands beside the oldest surviving minbar in the world, of carved teak, both believed to be imports from Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ribat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ribat.jpg" alt="Ribat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ribat">Ribat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TUNISIE_MONASTIR_RIBAT_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GIRAUD Patrick, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aghlabid army had two main elements: the jund, Arab troops descended from the tribesmen of the early conquests, and soldiers recruited from slaves, raised partly to counterbalance the jund. The jund rose in 824 under Mansur ibn Nasr al-Tunbudhi, who held a fortress near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>; by September the rebels had occupied Tunis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, and after Mansur was killed Amir ibn Nafi' inflicted a severe defeat on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-i">Ziyadat Allah I</a>. The emir prevailed only in 827, with the help of the Ibadite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of the Nafwaza region. In 838/839 the Ibadi province of Qastiliya revolted and Tozeur had to be retaken. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">Ribats</a> guarded the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a> and Monastir, and the Ribat of Sousse and the Ribat of Monastir still stand.</p><p>The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, begun in 827 under the qadi Asad ibn al-Furat at the invitation of the Byzantine rebel Euphemios, gave the restless jund a new outlet. A fleet from the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a> under Asba' ibn Wakil joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> despite their rivalry; Ibn Kathir records 300 ships in all. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> fell in 831 and became the base of Muslim rule; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> followed in 842 or 843, Syracuse in 878 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> in 902, though pockets of Christian resistance held out until 967. From 835 Aghlabid forces campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, taking Amantea, Taranto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, but the destruction of a Muslim fleet off Ostia in 849 halted the advance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> was conquered in 870.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/raqqada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raqqada.jpg" alt="Raqqada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bassins_Arabe_de_Raqada_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IssamBarhoumi, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The emir ruled as a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, whose name was spoken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a>. Residence shifted from al-Abbasiyya to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a>, the palace-city Ibrahim II founded in 876 with a mosque, baths, a market and several palaces, where he and his successors lived in the Qasr al-Fath except for periods spent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Succession passed within the family, from brother to brother as often as from father to son, and ended in violence: Abdallah II was assassinated on 27 July 903, a year after his accession.</p><p>Religion set the dynasty against its subjects. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> adopted the Mu'tazilite rationalist doctrine after the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> (813–833) made it official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and imposed it as the state doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> majority rejected its teaching that the Qur'an was created rather than God's eternal word. The qadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> who endorsed the createdness of the Qur'an, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> consistently favoured Iraqis as their higher-ranking judges, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> were Malikis. Unable and unwilling to alter their government to suit the Maliki leaders, the emirs instead drew them into the orbit of the state with appointments to high religious offices, and persecuted some who resisted, such as Sahsun under Muhammad I (841–856). Abroad, they kept strained relations with the Ibadi Rustamids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiaret">Tahert</a>, tense relations with the Zaydi Shi'ite Idrisids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, and a hostile stance towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/palermo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palermo.jpg" alt="Palermo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sicilia_Palermo5_tango7174.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tango7174, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> across the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Their conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, pursued from west to east over 75 years, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> the capital of Muslim rule on the island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> a base for the Italian mainland; a Muslim force raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 846, though it is not certain that it came from Aghlabid territory. Many of the Muslim bands on the peninsula had only tenuous allegiance to the dynasty, serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> rulers or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radelchis-i-of-benevento">Radelchis I of Benevento</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> (847–871) had rulers of its own. Whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> was ever occupied is disputed: Corrado Zedda argues for a limited coastal foothold, while Alex Metcalfe and, according to Fabio Pinna, most Sardinian historians and archaeologists hold that the evidence supports only raids.</p><p>At home the dynasty left some of the oldest Islamic-era monuments of the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a>, the Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a>, the Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">ribats</a> of Sousse and Monastir and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> survive to the present, and the Kairouan mosque's dome, carved minbar and lustre-tiled mihrab remain landmarks of early Islamic art. The Aghlabids also fixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>'s religious politics: their Mu'tazilite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> establishment, set against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> majority, and their reliance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> slave soldiers against the Arab jund, shaped the province the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> inherited in 909, and the conquest of Sicily they began outlived them by more than a century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii.jpg" alt="Ziyadat Allah III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii">Ziyadat Allah III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aghlabid_dinar_-_Ziyadat_Allah_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DrFO.Jr.Tn, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Decline began under Ibrahim II (875–902), who had to repel an attack by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and put down a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> revolt. In 893 the Isma'ili Fatimid movement began among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a>. In 902 Ibrahim II became the only Aghlabid emir to lead a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in person; while he was away Abu Abdallah captured Mila, and a punitive expedition of 12,000 men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> drove him from Tazrut to Ikjan. Ibrahim died in October 902 besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosenza">Cosenza</a>. His son Abdallah II was assassinated on 27 July 903, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyadat-allah-iii">Ziyadat Allah III</a> took power in Tunis.</p><p>These troubles let Abu Abdallah retake Mila and capture Setif by late 904, and later Aghlabid attempts to crush him had little success. In 907 Ziyadat Allah III moved his court back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a> and fortified it, but the heavily fortified city of Baghaya, on the Roman road into the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, fell to the Kutama and opened a hole in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>'s defences. A battle near Dar Madyan in 908 was indecisive. On 25 February 909 Abu Abdallah marched from Ikjan with an army put at 200,000 men. Near al-Aribus on 18 March the Kutama horsemen outflanked the Aghlabid army under the prince Ibrahim Ibn Abi al-Aghlab and routed it. Ziyadat Allah III packed his treasures and fled towards Egypt, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> refused a last stand, and on 25 March 909 Abu Abdallah entered the city. That year his forces brought Abdallah al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> to Ifriqiya and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Aghlabid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> from 800 to 909 as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, raising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribat">ribats</a> of the coast, and imposing Mu'tazilite doctrine on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> majority, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> of the Fatimid movement routed its army and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a>.</em></p>

